1
00:00:01,796 --> 00:00:05,116
Chapter 54.

2
00:00:05,116 --> 00:00:10,000
It was one of those March days when the sun shines hot and the wind blows cold:

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,597
when it is summer in the light, and winter in the shade. We had our pea-coats with us,

4
00:00:15,597 --> 00:00:21,237
and I took a bag. Of all my worldly possessions I took no more than the few

5
00:00:21,237 --> 00:00:27,796
necessaries that filled the bag. Where I might go, what I might do, or when I might return,

6
00:00:27,796 --> 00:00:34,078
were questions utterly unknown to me; nor did I vex my mind with them, for it was wholly set on

7
00:00:34,078 --> 00:00:41,358
Provis’s safety. I only wondered for the passing moment, as I stopped at the door and looked back,

8
00:00:41,358 --> 00:00:48,078
under what altered circumstances I should next see those rooms, if ever.

9
00:00:48,078 --> 00:00:53,957
We loitered down to the Temple stairs, and stood loitering there, as if we were not quite decided

10
00:00:53,957 --> 00:00:59,480
to go upon the water at all. Of course, I had taken care that the boat should be ready and

11
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:05,038
everything in order. After a little show of indecision, which there were none to see but

12
00:01:05,038 --> 00:01:11,116
the two or three amphibious creatures belonging to our Temple stairs, we went on board and cast off;

13
00:01:11,116 --> 00:01:17,718
Herbert in the bow, I steering. It was then about high-water,—half-past eight.

14
00:01:17,718 --> 00:01:24,078
Our plan was this. The tide, beginning to run down at nine, and being with us until three,

15
00:01:24,078 --> 00:01:28,436
we intended still to creep on after it had turned, and row against it until

16
00:01:28,438 --> 00:01:34,796
dark. We should then be well in those long reaches below Gravesend, between Kent and Essex,

17
00:01:34,796 --> 00:01:40,839
where the river is broad and solitary, where the water-side inhabitants are very few, and where

18
00:01:40,839 --> 00:01:46,839
lone public-houses are scattered here and there, of which we could choose one for a resting-place.

19
00:01:46,839 --> 00:01:53,358
There, we meant to lie by all night. The steamer for Hamburg and the steamer for Rotterdam would

20
00:01:53,358 --> 00:01:59,436
start from London at about nine on Thursday morning. We should know at what time to expect

21
00:01:59,438 --> 00:02:05,438
them, according to where we were, and would hail the first; so that, if by any accident we were

22
00:02:05,438 --> 00:02:12,116
not taken abroad, we should have another chance. We knew the distinguishing marks of each vessel.

23
00:02:12,116 --> 00:02:17,637
The relief of being at last engaged in the execution of the purpose was so great to me

24
00:02:17,637 --> 00:02:23,160
that I felt it difficult to realise the condition in which I had been a few hours before. The crisp

25
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:28,637
air, the sunlight, the movement on the river, and the moving river itself,—the

26
00:02:28,637 --> 00:02:36,758
road that ran with us, seeming to sympathise with us, animate us, and encourage us on,—freshened me

27
00:02:36,758 --> 00:02:43,038
with new hope. I felt mortified to be of so little use in the boat; but, there were few

28
00:02:43,038 --> 00:02:50,319
better oarsmen than my two friends, and they rowed with a steady stroke that was to last all day.

29
00:02:50,319 --> 00:02:55,078
At that time, the steam-traffic on the Thames was far below its present extent,

30
00:02:55,078 --> 00:03:01,480
and watermen’s boats were far more numerous. Of barges, sailing colliers, and coasting-traders,

31
00:03:01,480 --> 00:03:06,277
there were perhaps, as many as now; but of steam-ships, great and small,

32
00:03:06,277 --> 00:03:12,557
not a tithe or a twentieth part so many. Early as it was, there were plenty of scullers going

33
00:03:12,559 --> 00:03:17,480
here and there that morning, and plenty of barges dropping down with the tide;

34
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:22,917
the navigation of the river between bridges, in an open boat, was a much easier and commoner matter

35
00:03:22,917 --> 00:03:30,078
in those days than it is in these; and we went ahead among many skiffs and wherries briskly.

36
00:03:30,078 --> 00:03:34,116
Old London Bridge was soon passed, and old Billingsgate Market with

37
00:03:34,116 --> 00:03:38,436
its oyster-boats and Dutchmen, and the White Tower and Traitor’s Gate,

38
00:03:38,438 --> 00:03:44,398
and we were in among the tiers of shipping. Here were the Leith, Aberdeen, and Glasgow steamers,

39
00:03:44,398 --> 00:03:50,519
loading and unloading goods, and looking immensely high out of the water as we passed alongside;

40
00:03:50,519 --> 00:03:55,718
here, were colliers by the score and score, with the coal-whippers plunging off stages on deck,

41
00:03:55,718 --> 00:04:02,519
as counterweights to measures of coal swinging up, which were then rattled over the side into barges;

42
00:04:02,519 --> 00:04:08,160
here, at her moorings was to-morrow’s steamer for Rotterdam, of which we took good notice;

43
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:14,718
and here to-morrow’s for Hamburg, under whose bowsprit we crossed. And now I, sitting in

44
00:04:14,718 --> 00:04:22,519
the stern, could see, with a faster beating heart, Mill Pond Bank and Mill Pond stairs.

45
00:04:22,519 --> 00:04:24,597
“Is he there?” said Herbert.

46
00:04:24,597 --> 00:04:25,879
“Not yet.”

47
00:04:25,879 --> 00:04:30,637
“Right! He was not to come down till he saw us. Can you see his signal?”

48
00:04:30,637 --> 00:04:37,519
“Not well from here; but I think I see it.—Now I see him! Pull both. Easy, Herbert. Oars!”

49
00:04:37,519 --> 00:04:40,116
We touched the stairs lightly for a single moment,

50
00:04:40,116 --> 00:04:44,480
and he was on board, and we were off again. He had a boat-cloak with him,

51
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:49,917
and a black canvas bag; and he looked as like a river-pilot as my heart could have wished.

52
00:04:49,917 --> 00:04:54,718
“Dear boy!” he said, putting his arm on my shoulder, as he took his seat.

53
00:04:54,718 --> 00:05:02,319
“Faithful dear boy, well done. Thankye, thankye!” Again among the tiers of shipping, in and out,

54
00:05:02,319 --> 00:05:08,597
avoiding rusty chain-cables frayed hempen hawsers and bobbing buoys, sinking for the moment floating

55
00:05:08,597 --> 00:05:15,358
broken baskets, scattering floating chips of wood and shaving, cleaving floating scum of coal,

56
00:05:15,358 --> 00:05:20,879
in and out, under the figure-head of the John of Sunderland making a speech to the winds (as is

57
00:05:20,879 --> 00:05:27,199
done by many Johns), and the Betsy of Yarmouth with a firm formality of bosom and her knobby

58
00:05:27,199 --> 00:05:33,557
eyes starting two inches out of her head; in and out, hammers going in ship-builders’ yards, saws

59
00:05:33,559 --> 00:05:39,839
going at timber, clashing engines going at things unknown, pumps going in leaky ships, capstans

60
00:05:39,839 --> 00:05:45,758
going, ships going out to sea, and unintelligible sea-creatures roaring curses over the bulwarks at

61
00:05:45,758 --> 00:05:51,637
respondent lightermen, in and out,—out at last upon the clearer river, where the ships’ boys

62
00:05:51,637 --> 00:05:57,000
might take their fenders in, no longer fishing in troubled waters with them over the side, and where

63
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:03,637
the festooned sails might fly out to the wind. At the stairs where we had taken him abroad, and

64
00:06:03,637 --> 00:06:10,597
ever since, I had looked warily for any token of our being suspected. I had seen none. We certainly

65
00:06:10,597 --> 00:06:17,000
had not been, and at that time as certainly we were not either attended or followed by any boat.

66
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:22,078
If we had been waited on by any boat, I should have run in to shore, and have obliged her to go

67
00:06:22,078 --> 00:06:27,917
on, or to make her purpose evident. But we held our own without any appearance of molestation.

68
00:06:28,917 --> 00:06:35,078
He had his boat-cloak on him, and looked, as I have said, a natural part of the scene. It

69
00:06:35,078 --> 00:06:40,436
was remarkable (but perhaps the wretched life he had led accounted for it) that he was the least

70
00:06:40,438 --> 00:06:46,358
anxious of any of us. He was not indifferent, for he told me that he hoped to live to see

71
00:06:46,358 --> 00:06:52,116
his gentleman one of the best of gentlemen in a foreign country; he was not disposed to be

72
00:06:52,116 --> 00:06:57,839
passive or resigned, as I understood it; but he had no notion of meeting danger half way.

73
00:06:58,718 --> 00:07:04,358
When it came upon him, he confronted it, but it must come before he troubled himself.

74
00:07:04,358 --> 00:07:07,116
“If you knowed, dear boy,” he said to me,

75
00:07:07,116 --> 00:07:10,758
“what it is to sit here alonger my dear boy and have my smoke,

76
00:07:10,758 --> 00:07:17,559
arter having been day by day betwixt four walls, you’d envy me. But you don’t know what it is.”

77
00:07:17,559 --> 00:07:21,160
“I think I know the delights of freedom,” I answered.

78
00:07:21,160 --> 00:07:27,597
“Ah,” said he, shaking his head gravely. “But you don’t know it equal to me. You must have

79
00:07:27,597 --> 00:07:33,917
been under lock and key, dear boy, to know it equal to me,—but I ain’t a-going to be low.”

80
00:07:33,917 --> 00:07:37,637
It occurred to me as inconsistent, that, for any mastering idea,

81
00:07:37,637 --> 00:07:42,680
he should have endangered his freedom, and even his life. But I reflected that perhaps

82
00:07:42,680 --> 00:07:47,277
freedom without danger was too much apart from all the habit of his existence to be

83
00:07:47,277 --> 00:07:54,557
to him what it would be to another man. I was not far out, since he said, after smoking a little:—

84
00:07:54,559 --> 00:07:59,597
“You see, dear boy, when I was over yonder, t’other side the world,

85
00:07:59,597 --> 00:08:04,116
I was always a looking to this side; and it come flat to be there,

86
00:08:04,116 --> 00:08:09,879
for all I was a growing rich. Everybody knowed Magwitch, and Magwitch could come,

87
00:08:09,879 --> 00:08:15,038
and Magwitch could go, and nobody’s head would be troubled about him. They ain’t so

88
00:08:15,038 --> 00:08:21,358
easy concerning me here, dear boy,—wouldn’t be, leastwise, if they knowed where I was.”

89
00:08:21,358 --> 00:08:27,917
“If all goes well,” said I, “you will be perfectly free and safe again within a few hours.”

90
00:08:27,917 --> 00:08:32,680
“Well,” he returned, drawing a long breath, “I hope so.”

91
00:08:32,680 --> 00:08:34,237
“And think so?”

92
00:08:34,237 --> 00:08:37,796
He dipped his hand in the water over the boat’s gunwale, and said,

93
00:08:37,796 --> 00:08:41,298
smiling with that softened air upon him which was not new to me:—

94
00:08:41,298 --> 00:08:47,398
“Ay, I s’pose I think so, dear boy. We’d be puzzled to be more quiet and easy-going than

95
00:08:47,398 --> 00:08:51,678
we are at present. But—it’s a flowing so soft and pleasant through the water,

96
00:08:51,680 --> 00:08:57,000
p’raps, as makes me think it—I was a thinking through my smoke just then,

97
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:01,358
that we can no more see to the bottom of the next few hours than we can see to the bottom

98
00:09:01,358 --> 00:09:07,838
of this river what I catches hold of. Nor yet we can’t no more hold their tide than I can

99
00:09:07,839 --> 00:09:14,438
hold this. And it’s run through my fingers and gone, you see!” holding up his dripping hand.

100
00:09:14,438 --> 00:09:18,160
“But for your face I should think you were a little despondent,” said I.

101
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:23,476
“Not a bit on it, dear boy! It comes of flowing on so quiet, and of that there rippling at the

102
00:09:23,476 --> 00:09:30,038
boat’s head making a sort of a Sunday tune. Maybe I’m a growing a trifle old besides.”

103
00:09:30,038 --> 00:09:34,476
He put his pipe back in his mouth with an undisturbed expression of face,

104
00:09:34,476 --> 00:09:40,680
and sat as composed and contented as if we were already out of England. Yet he was as

105
00:09:40,680 --> 00:09:46,718
submissive to a word of advice as if he had been in constant terror; for, when we ran ashore to

106
00:09:46,718 --> 00:09:51,476
get some bottles of beer into the boat, and he was stepping out, I hinted that I thought

107
00:09:51,476 --> 00:09:57,637
he would be safest where he was, and he said. “Do you, dear boy?” and quietly sat down again.

108
00:09:59,199 --> 00:10:05,199
The air felt cold upon the river, but it was a bright day, and the sunshine was very cheering.

109
00:10:05,199 --> 00:10:11,078
The tide ran strong, I took care to lose none of it, and our steady stroke carried us on thoroughly

110
00:10:11,078 --> 00:10:18,160
well. By imperceptible degrees, as the tide ran out, we lost more and more of the nearer woods

111
00:10:18,160 --> 00:10:24,038
and hills, and dropped lower and lower between the muddy banks, but the tide was yet with us

112
00:10:24,038 --> 00:10:30,519
when we were off Gravesend. As our charge was wrapped in his cloak, I purposely passed within

113
00:10:30,519 --> 00:10:36,839
a boat or two’s length of the floating Custom House, and so out to catch the stream, alongside

114
00:10:36,839 --> 00:10:42,519
of two emigrant ships, and under the bows of a large transport with troops on the forecastle

115
00:10:42,519 --> 00:10:49,796
looking down at us. And soon the tide began to slacken, and the craft lying at anchor to swing,

116
00:10:49,796 --> 00:10:54,637
and presently they had all swung round, and the ships that were taking advantage of the new tide

117
00:10:54,637 --> 00:11:01,476
to get up to the Pool began to crowd upon us in a fleet, and we kept under the shore, as much out of

118
00:11:01,476 --> 00:11:09,160
the strength of the tide now as we could, standing carefully off from low shallows and mudbanks.

119
00:11:09,160 --> 00:11:14,398
Our oarsmen were so fresh, by dint of having occasionally let her drive with the tide for

120
00:11:14,398 --> 00:11:20,319
a minute or two, that a quarter of an hour’s rest proved full as much as they wanted. We

121
00:11:20,319 --> 00:11:24,718
got ashore among some slippery stones while we ate and drank what we had with us, and

122
00:11:24,718 --> 00:11:32,438
looked about. It was like my own marsh country, flat and monotonous, and with a dim horizon;

123
00:11:32,438 --> 00:11:39,237
while the winding river turned and turned, and the great floating buoys upon it turned and turned,

124
00:11:39,237 --> 00:11:45,557
and everything else seemed stranded and still. For now the last of the fleet of ships was round

125
00:11:45,559 --> 00:11:51,559
the last low point we had headed; and the last green barge, straw-laden, with a brown sail,

126
00:11:51,559 --> 00:11:57,637
had followed; and some ballast-lighters, shaped like a child’s first rude imitation of a boat,

127
00:11:57,637 --> 00:12:03,597
lay low in the mud; and a little squat shoal-lighthouse on open piles stood crippled in

128
00:12:03,597 --> 00:12:10,680
the mud on stilts and crutches; and slimy stakes stuck out of the mud, and slimy stones stuck out

129
00:12:10,680 --> 00:12:16,917
of the mud, and red landmarks and tidemarks stuck out of the mud, and an old landing-stage

130
00:12:16,917 --> 00:12:23,879
and an old roofless building slipped into the mud, and all about us was stagnation and mud.

131
00:12:23,879 --> 00:12:28,839
We pushed off again, and made what way we could. It was much harder work now,

132
00:12:28,839 --> 00:12:35,839
but Herbert and Startop persevered, and rowed and rowed and rowed until the sun went down. By that

133
00:12:35,839 --> 00:12:42,038
time the river had lifted us a little, so that we could see above the bank. There was the red sun,

134
00:12:42,038 --> 00:12:47,718
on the low level of the shore, in a purple haze, fast deepening into black;

135
00:12:47,718 --> 00:12:54,038
and there was the solitary flat marsh; and far away there were the rising grounds, between which

136
00:12:54,038 --> 00:13:01,519
and us there seemed to be no life, save here and there in the foreground a melancholy gull.

137
00:13:01,519 --> 00:13:07,879
As the night was fast falling, and as the moon, being past the full, would not rise early,

138
00:13:07,879 --> 00:13:13,758
we held a little council; a short one, for clearly our course was to lie by at the first

139
00:13:13,758 --> 00:13:19,957
lonely tavern we could find. So, they plied their oars once more, and I looked out for anything like

140
00:13:19,957 --> 00:13:29,277
a house. Thus we held on, speaking little, for four or five dull miles. It was very cold, and, a

141
00:13:29,277 --> 00:13:35,756
collier coming by us, with her galley-fire smoking and flaring, looked like a comfortable home. The

142
00:13:35,758 --> 00:13:41,038
night was as dark by this time as it would be until morning; and what light we had, seemed to

143
00:13:41,038 --> 00:13:47,796
come more from the river than the sky, as the oars in their dipping struck at a few reflected stars.

144
00:13:47,796 --> 00:13:53,319
At this dismal time we were evidently all possessed by the idea that we were followed.

145
00:13:53,319 --> 00:13:57,436
As the tide made, it flapped heavily at irregular intervals against the shore;

146
00:13:58,398 --> 00:14:02,476
and whenever such a sound came, one or other of us was sure to start,

147
00:14:02,476 --> 00:14:07,519
and look in that direction. Here and there, the set of the current had worn down the bank

148
00:14:07,519 --> 00:14:14,839
into a little creek, and we were all suspicious of such places, and eyed them nervously. Sometimes,

149
00:14:14,839 --> 00:14:19,597
“What was that ripple?” one of us would say in a low voice. Or another,

150
00:14:19,597 --> 00:14:24,917
“Is that a boat yonder?” And afterwards we would fall into a dead silence, and I would

151
00:14:24,917 --> 00:14:31,237
sit impatiently thinking with what an unusual amount of noise the oars worked in the thowels.

152
00:14:31,237 --> 00:14:36,319
At length we descried a light and a roof, and presently afterwards ran alongside a little

153
00:14:36,319 --> 00:14:42,678
causeway made of stones that had been picked up hard by. Leaving the rest in the boat, I stepped

154
00:14:42,680 --> 00:14:49,519
ashore, and found the light to be in a window of a public-house. It was a dirty place enough, and I

155
00:14:49,519 --> 00:14:54,758
dare say not unknown to smuggling adventurers; but there was a good fire in the kitchen, and there

156
00:14:54,758 --> 00:15:01,199
were eggs and bacon to eat, and various liquors to drink. Also, there were two double-bedded

157
00:15:01,199 --> 00:15:07,319
rooms,—“such as they were,” the landlord said. No other company was in the house than the landlord,

158
00:15:07,319 --> 00:15:12,957
his wife, and a grizzled male creature, the “Jack” of the little causeway, who was as slimy and

159
00:15:12,957 --> 00:15:19,237
smeary as if he had been low-water mark too. With this assistant, I went down to the boat

160
00:15:19,237 --> 00:15:25,718
again, and we all came ashore, and brought out the oars, and rudder and boat-hook, and all else,

161
00:15:25,718 --> 00:15:31,319
and hauled her up for the night. We made a very good meal by the kitchen fire, and then

162
00:15:31,319 --> 00:15:38,160
apportioned the bedrooms: Herbert and Startop were to occupy one; I and our charge the other.

163
00:15:38,160 --> 00:15:44,078
We found the air as carefully excluded from both, as if air were fatal to life; and there were more

164
00:15:44,078 --> 00:15:49,796
dirty clothes and bandboxes under the beds than I should have thought the family possessed. But

165
00:15:49,796 --> 00:15:56,917
we considered ourselves well off, notwithstanding, for a more solitary place we could not have found.

166
00:15:56,917 --> 00:16:01,917
While we were comforting ourselves by the fire after our meal, the Jack—who was sitting in a

167
00:16:01,917 --> 00:16:07,199
corner, and who had a bloated pair of shoes on, which he had exhibited while we were eating our

168
00:16:07,199 --> 00:16:12,597
eggs and bacon, as interesting relics that he had taken a few days ago from the feet of a drowned

169
00:16:12,597 --> 00:16:18,559
seaman washed ashore—asked me if we had seen a four-oared galley going up with the tide? When I

170
00:16:18,559 --> 00:16:25,758
told him No, he said she must have gone down then, and yet she “took up too,” when she left there.

171
00:16:25,758 --> 00:16:31,000
“They must ha’ thought better on’t for some reason or another,” said the Jack, “and gone down.”

172
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,957
“A four-oared galley, did you say?” said I.

173
00:16:33,957 --> 00:16:37,758
“A four,” said the Jack, “and two sitters.”

174
00:16:37,758 --> 00:16:39,519
“Did they come ashore here?”

175
00:16:39,519 --> 00:16:43,758
“They put in with a stone two-gallon jar for some beer. I’d ha’ been glad to

176
00:16:43,758 --> 00:16:49,000
pison the beer myself,” said the Jack, “or put some rattling physic in it.”

177
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:50,680
“Why?”

178
00:16:50,680 --> 00:16:59,476
“I know why,” said the Jack. He spoke in a slushy voice, as if much mud had washed into his throat.

179
00:16:59,476 --> 00:17:04,116
“He thinks,” said the landlord, a weakly meditative man with a pale eye,

180
00:17:04,116 --> 00:17:09,957
who seemed to rely greatly on his Jack,—“he thinks they was, what they wasn’t.”

181
00:17:09,957 --> 00:17:13,436
“I knows what I thinks,” observed the Jack.

182
00:17:13,438 --> 00:17:17,078
“You thinks Custom ’Us, Jack?” said the landlord.

183
00:17:17,078 --> 00:17:19,398
“I do,” said the Jack.

184
00:17:19,398 --> 00:17:21,277
“Then you’re wrong, Jack.”

185
00:17:21,277 --> 00:17:23,116
“AM I!”

186
00:17:23,116 --> 00:17:27,160
In the infinite meaning of his reply and his boundless confidence in his views,

187
00:17:27,718 --> 00:17:31,358
the Jack took one of his bloated shoes off, looked into it,

188
00:17:31,358 --> 00:17:35,957
knocked a few stones out of it on the kitchen floor, and put it on again. He

189
00:17:35,957 --> 00:17:41,319
did this with the air of a Jack who was so right that he could afford to do anything.

190
00:17:41,319 --> 00:17:44,237
“Why, what do you make out that they done with their buttons then,

191
00:17:44,237 --> 00:17:47,758
Jack?” asked the landlord, vacillating weakly.

192
00:17:47,758 --> 00:17:52,358
“Done with their buttons?” returned the Jack. “Chucked ’em overboard. Swallered

193
00:17:52,358 --> 00:17:57,476
’em. Sowed ’em, to come up small salad. Done with their buttons!”

194
00:17:57,476 --> 00:18:03,160
“Don’t be cheeky, Jack,” remonstrated the landlord, in a melancholy and pathetic way.

195
00:18:03,160 --> 00:18:07,319
“A Custom ’Us officer knows what to do with his Buttons,” said the Jack,

196
00:18:07,319 --> 00:18:11,319
repeating the obnoxious word with the greatest contempt, “when they comes

197
00:18:11,319 --> 00:18:17,038
betwixt him and his own light. A four and two sitters don’t go hanging and hovering,

198
00:18:17,038 --> 00:18:21,398
up with one tide and down with another, and both with and against another,

199
00:18:21,398 --> 00:18:27,237
without there being Custom ’Us at the bottom of it.” Saying which he went out in disdain;

200
00:18:27,237 --> 00:18:33,319
and the landlord, having no one to reply upon, found it impracticable to pursue the subject.

201
00:18:33,319 --> 00:18:40,160
This dialogue made us all uneasy, and me very uneasy. The dismal wind was muttering round the

202
00:18:40,160 --> 00:18:45,476
house, the tide was flapping at the shore, and I had a feeling that we were caged and

203
00:18:45,476 --> 00:18:52,358
threatened. A four-oared galley hovering about in so unusual a way as to attract this notice

204
00:18:52,358 --> 00:19:00,557
was an ugly circumstance that I could not get rid of. When I had induced Provis to go up to bed,

205
00:19:00,559 --> 00:19:06,438
I went outside with my two companions (Startop by this time knew the state of the case),

206
00:19:06,438 --> 00:19:13,277
and held another council. Whether we should remain at the house until near the steamer’s time,

207
00:19:13,277 --> 00:19:18,116
which would be about one in the afternoon, or whether we should put off early in the morning,

208
00:19:18,116 --> 00:19:24,116
was the question we discussed. On the whole we deemed it the better course to lie where we were,

209
00:19:24,116 --> 00:19:29,277
until within an hour or so of the steamer’s time, and then to get out in her track, and

210
00:19:29,277 --> 00:19:37,237
drift easily with the tide. Having settled to do this, we returned into the house and went to bed.

211
00:19:37,237 --> 00:19:43,597
I lay down with the greater part of my clothes on, and slept well for a few hours. When I awoke,

212
00:19:43,597 --> 00:19:50,116
the wind had risen, and the sign of the house (the Ship) was creaking and banging about, with noises

213
00:19:50,116 --> 00:19:57,038
that startled me. Rising softly, for my charge lay fast asleep, I looked out of the window.

214
00:19:57,597 --> 00:20:02,839
It commanded the causeway where we had hauled up our boat, and, as my eyes adapted themselves

215
00:20:02,839 --> 00:20:09,277
to the light of the clouded moon, I saw two men looking into her. They passed by under the window,

216
00:20:09,277 --> 00:20:13,917
looking at nothing else, and they did not go down to the landing-place which I could

217
00:20:13,917 --> 00:20:19,957
discern to be empty, but struck across the marsh in the direction of the Nore.

218
00:20:19,957 --> 00:20:26,957
My first impulse was to call up Herbert, and show him the two men going away. But reflecting,

219
00:20:26,957 --> 00:20:31,559
before I got into his room, which was at the back of the house and adjoined mine,

220
00:20:31,559 --> 00:20:38,718
that he and Startop had had a harder day than I, and were fatigued, I forbore. Going back to

221
00:20:38,718 --> 00:20:44,277
my window, I could see the two men moving over the marsh. In that light, however,

222
00:20:44,277 --> 00:20:52,237
I soon lost them, and, feeling very cold, lay down to think of the matter, and fell asleep again.

223
00:20:52,237 --> 00:20:56,917
We were up early. As we walked to and fro, all four together, before breakfast,

224
00:20:57,796 --> 00:21:02,519
I deemed it right to recount what I had seen. Again our charge was the least

225
00:21:02,519 --> 00:21:07,559
anxious of the party. It was very likely that the men belonged to the Custom House,

226
00:21:07,559 --> 00:21:15,160
he said quietly, and that they had no thought of us. I tried to persuade myself that it was so,—as,

227
00:21:15,160 --> 00:21:19,879
indeed, it might easily be. However, I proposed that he and I should walk

228
00:21:19,879 --> 00:21:25,358
away together to a distant point we could see, and that the boat should take us aboard there,

229
00:21:25,358 --> 00:21:32,718
or as near there as might prove feasible, at about noon. This being considered a good precaution,

230
00:21:32,718 --> 00:21:38,718
soon after breakfast he and I set forth, without saying anything at the tavern.

231
00:21:38,718 --> 00:21:44,559
He smoked his pipe as we went along, and sometimes stopped to clap me on the shoulder. One would have

232
00:21:44,559 --> 00:21:50,519
supposed that it was I who was in danger, not he, and that he was reassuring me. We spoke

233
00:21:50,519 --> 00:21:57,038
very little. As we approached the point, I begged him to remain in a sheltered place, while I went

234
00:21:57,038 --> 00:22:03,758
on to reconnoitre; for it was towards it that the men had passed in the night. He complied,

235
00:22:03,758 --> 00:22:11,038
and I went on alone. There was no boat off the point, nor any boat drawn up anywhere near it,

236
00:22:11,038 --> 00:22:14,839
nor were there any signs of the men having embarked there. But,

237
00:22:14,839 --> 00:22:20,718
to be sure, the tide was high, and there might have been some footprints under water.

238
00:22:20,718 --> 00:22:24,796
When he looked out from his shelter in the distance, and saw that I waved my hat to

239
00:22:24,796 --> 00:22:31,597
him to come up, he rejoined me, and there we waited; sometimes lying on the bank,

240
00:22:31,597 --> 00:22:37,839
wrapped in our coats, and sometimes moving about to warm ourselves, until we saw our boat coming

241
00:22:37,839 --> 00:22:44,597
round. We got aboard easily, and rowed out into the track of the steamer. By that time

242
00:22:44,597 --> 00:22:49,917
it wanted but ten minutes of one o’clock, and we began to look out for her smoke.

243
00:22:49,917 --> 00:22:55,319
But, it was half-past one before we saw her smoke, and soon afterwards we saw behind it the

244
00:22:55,319 --> 00:23:01,519
smoke of another steamer. As they were coming on at full speed, we got the two bags ready,

245
00:23:01,519 --> 00:23:07,319
and took that opportunity of saying good-bye to Herbert and Startop. We had all shaken hands

246
00:23:07,319 --> 00:23:12,917
cordially, and neither Herbert’s eyes nor mine were quite dry, when I saw a four-oared galley

247
00:23:12,917 --> 00:23:19,358
shoot out from under the bank but a little way ahead of us, and row out into the same track.

248
00:23:19,358 --> 00:23:24,476
A stretch of shore had been as yet between us and the steamer’s smoke, by reason of the bend and

249
00:23:24,476 --> 00:23:30,758
wind of the river; but now she was visible, coming head on. I called to Herbert and Startop to keep

250
00:23:30,758 --> 00:23:36,758
before the tide, that she might see us lying by for her, and I adjured Provis to sit quite still,

251
00:23:36,758 --> 00:23:45,000
wrapped in his cloak. He answered cheerily, “Trust to me, dear boy,” and sat like a statue.

252
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:50,758
Meantime the galley, which was very skilfully handled, had crossed us, let us come up with her,

253
00:23:50,758 --> 00:23:57,038
and fallen alongside. Leaving just room enough for the play of the oars, she kept alongside,

254
00:23:57,038 --> 00:24:03,559
drifting when we drifted, and pulling a stroke or two when we pulled. Of the two sitters one

255
00:24:03,559 --> 00:24:09,398
held the rudder-lines, and looked at us attentively,—as did all the rowers;

256
00:24:09,398 --> 00:24:14,358
the other sitter was wrapped up, much as Provis was, and seemed to shrink, and whisper

257
00:24:14,358 --> 00:24:20,398
some instruction to the steerer as he looked at us. Not a word was spoken in either boat.

258
00:24:20,398 --> 00:24:25,078
Startop could make out, after a few minutes, which steamer was first,

259
00:24:25,078 --> 00:24:33,319
and gave me the word “Hamburg,” in a low voice, as we sat face to face. She was nearing us very fast,

260
00:24:33,319 --> 00:24:37,796
and the beating of her peddles grew louder and louder. I felt as if her

261
00:24:37,796 --> 00:24:44,078
shadow were absolutely upon us, when the galley hailed us. I answered.

262
00:24:44,078 --> 00:24:49,199
“You have a returned Transport there,” said the man who held the lines. “That’s the man,

263
00:24:49,199 --> 00:24:55,957
wrapped in the cloak. His name is Abel Magwitch, otherwise Provis. I apprehend that man, and call

264
00:24:55,957 --> 00:25:02,116
upon him to surrender, and you to assist.” At the same moment, without giving any audible

265
00:25:02,116 --> 00:25:08,519
direction to his crew, he ran the galley abroad of us. They had pulled one sudden stroke ahead,

266
00:25:08,519 --> 00:25:13,438
had got their oars in, had run athwart us, and were holding on to our gunwale,

267
00:25:13,438 --> 00:25:19,680
before we knew what they were doing. This caused great confusion on board the steamer, and I

268
00:25:19,680 --> 00:25:26,758
heard them calling to us, and heard the order given to stop the paddles, and heard them stop,

269
00:25:26,758 --> 00:25:33,358
but felt her driving down upon us irresistibly. In the same moment, I saw the steersman of the galley

270
00:25:33,358 --> 00:25:38,436
lay his hand on his prisoner’s shoulder, and saw that both boats were swinging round with the force

271
00:25:38,438 --> 00:25:44,796
of the tide, and saw that all hands on board the steamer were running forward quite frantically.

272
00:25:44,796 --> 00:25:50,879
Still, in the same moment, I saw the prisoner start up, lean across his captor, and pull the

273
00:25:50,879 --> 00:25:57,319
cloak from the neck of the shrinking sitter in the galley. Still in the same moment, I saw that

274
00:25:57,319 --> 00:26:04,398
the face disclosed, was the face of the other convict of long ago. Still, in the same moment,

275
00:26:04,398 --> 00:26:11,000
I saw the face tilt backward with a white terror on it that I shall never forget, and heard a great

276
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:18,116
cry on board the steamer, and a loud splash in the water, and felt the boat sink from under me.

277
00:26:18,116 --> 00:26:22,557
It was but for an instant that I seemed to struggle with a thousand mill-weirs and a

278
00:26:22,559 --> 00:26:30,319
thousand flashes of light; that instant past, I was taken on board the galley. Herbert was there,

279
00:26:30,319 --> 00:26:36,597
and Startop was there; but our boat was gone, and the two convicts were gone.

280
00:26:36,597 --> 00:26:41,237
What with the cries aboard the steamer, and the furious blowing off of her steam,

281
00:26:41,237 --> 00:26:47,319
and her driving on, and our driving on, I could not at first distinguish sky from

282
00:26:47,319 --> 00:26:53,277
water or shore from shore; but the crew of the galley righted her with great speed,

283
00:26:53,277 --> 00:27:00,000
and, pulling certain swift strong strokes ahead, lay upon their oars, every man looking silently

284
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:05,796
and eagerly at the water astern. Presently a dark object was seen in it, bearing towards

285
00:27:05,796 --> 00:27:13,237
us on the tide. No man spoke, but the steersman held up his hand, and all softly backed water,

286
00:27:13,237 --> 00:27:19,758
and kept the boat straight and true before it. As it came nearer, I saw it to be Magwitch, swimming,

287
00:27:19,758 --> 00:27:26,680
but not swimming freely. He was taken on board, and instantly manacled at the wrists and ankles.

288
00:27:26,680 --> 00:27:33,277
The galley was kept steady, and the silent, eager look-out at the water was resumed. But,

289
00:27:33,277 --> 00:27:38,917
the Rotterdam steamer now came up, and apparently not understanding what had happened, came on

290
00:27:38,917 --> 00:27:45,237
at speed. By the time she had been hailed and stopped, both steamers were drifting away from us,

291
00:27:45,237 --> 00:27:51,277
and we were rising and falling in a troubled wake of water. The look-out was kept, long after all

292
00:27:51,277 --> 00:27:56,436
was still again and the two steamers were gone; but everybody knew that it was hopeless now.

293
00:27:57,078 --> 00:28:02,758
At length we gave it up, and pulled under the shore towards the tavern we had lately left, where

294
00:28:02,758 --> 00:28:10,559
we were received with no little surprise. Here I was able to get some comforts for Magwitch,—Provis

295
00:28:10,559 --> 00:28:18,519
no longer,—who had received some very severe injury in the chest, and a deep cut in the head.

296
00:28:18,519 --> 00:28:22,438
He told me that he believed himself to have gone under the keel of the steamer,

297
00:28:22,438 --> 00:28:28,559
and to have been struck on the head in rising. The injury to his chest (which rendered his breathing

298
00:28:28,559 --> 00:28:33,796
extremely painful) he thought he had received against the side of the galley. He added that he

299
00:28:33,796 --> 00:28:39,038
did not pretend to say what he might or might not have done to Compeyson, but that, in the moment of

300
00:28:39,038 --> 00:28:45,597
his laying his hand on his cloak to identify him, that villain had staggered up and staggered back,

301
00:28:45,597 --> 00:28:51,718
and they had both gone overboard together, when the sudden wrenching of him (Magwitch) out of

302
00:28:51,718 --> 00:28:58,796
our boat, and the endeavour of his captor to keep him in it, had capsized us. He told me

303
00:28:58,796 --> 00:29:03,519
in a whisper that they had gone down fiercely locked in each other’s arms, and that there

304
00:29:03,519 --> 00:29:10,839
had been a struggle under water, and that he had disengaged himself, struck out, and swum away.

305
00:29:10,839 --> 00:29:16,319
I never had any reason to doubt the exact truth of what he thus told me.

306
00:29:16,319 --> 00:29:22,000
The officer who steered the galley gave the same account of their going overboard.

307
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:25,519
When I asked this officer’s permission to change the prisoner’s wet clothes by

308
00:29:25,519 --> 00:29:30,758
purchasing any spare garments I could get at the public-house, he gave it readily:

309
00:29:30,758 --> 00:29:35,917
merely observing that he must take charge of everything his prisoner had about him. So the

310
00:29:35,917 --> 00:29:42,000
pocket-book which had once been in my hands passed into the officer’s. He further gave me

311
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:50,038
leave to accompany the prisoner to London; but declined to accord that grace to my two friends.

312
00:29:50,038 --> 00:29:55,358
The Jack at the Ship was instructed where the drowned man had gone down, and undertook to

313
00:29:55,358 --> 00:30:00,597
search for the body in the places where it was likeliest to come ashore. His interest

314
00:30:00,597 --> 00:30:06,597
in its recovery seemed to me to be much heightened when he heard that it had stockings on. Probably,

315
00:30:06,597 --> 00:30:11,559
it took about a dozen drowned men to fit him out completely; and that may have been

316
00:30:11,559 --> 00:30:16,957
the reason why the different articles of his dress were in various stages of decay.

317
00:30:16,957 --> 00:30:21,879
We remained at the public-house until the tide turned, and then Magwitch was carried down to

318
00:30:21,879 --> 00:30:27,796
the galley and put on board. Herbert and Startop were to get to London by land, as soon as they

319
00:30:27,796 --> 00:30:34,398
could. We had a doleful parting, and when I took my place by Magwitch’s side, I felt

320
00:30:34,398 --> 00:30:42,277
that that was my place henceforth while he lived. For now, my repugnance to him had all melted away;

321
00:30:42,277 --> 00:30:46,957
and in the hunted, wounded, shackled creature who held my hand in his,

322
00:30:46,957 --> 00:30:51,559
I only saw a man who had meant to be my benefactor, and who had felt affectionately,

323
00:30:51,559 --> 00:30:56,239
gratefully, and generously, towards me with great constancy through a series of years.

324
00:30:57,879 --> 00:31:01,758
I only saw in him a much better man than I had been to Joe.

325
00:31:01,758 --> 00:31:06,000
His breathing became more difficult and painful as the night drew on, and often he

326
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:12,398
could not repress a groan. I tried to rest him on the arm I could use, in any easy position;

327
00:31:12,398 --> 00:31:16,838
but it was dreadful to think that I could not be sorry at heart for his being badly hurt,

328
00:31:16,839 --> 00:31:22,796
since it was unquestionably best that he should die. That there were, still living,

329
00:31:22,796 --> 00:31:28,559
people enough who were able and willing to identify him, I could not doubt. That he would

330
00:31:28,559 --> 00:31:37,199
be leniently treated, I could not hope. He who had been presented in the worst light at his trial,

331
00:31:37,199 --> 00:31:42,718
who had since broken prison and had been tried again, who had returned from transportation

332
00:31:42,718 --> 00:31:49,637
under a life sentence, and who had occasioned the death of the man who was the cause of his arrest.

333
00:31:49,637 --> 00:31:55,000
As we returned towards the setting sun we had yesterday left behind us,

334
00:31:55,000 --> 00:31:58,680
and as the stream of our hopes seemed all running back,

335
00:31:58,680 --> 00:32:03,839
I told him how grieved I was to think that he had come home for my sake.

336
00:32:03,839 --> 00:32:10,680
“Dear boy,” he answered, “I’m quite content to take my chance. I’ve seen my boy,

337
00:32:10,680 --> 00:32:14,038
and he can be a gentleman without me.”

338
00:32:14,038 --> 00:32:21,160
No. I had thought about that, while we had been there side by side. No. Apart from any

339
00:32:21,160 --> 00:32:26,277
inclinations of my own, I understood Wemmick’s hint now. I foresaw that,

340
00:32:26,277 --> 00:32:30,358
being convicted, his possessions would be forfeited to the Crown.

341
00:32:30,358 --> 00:32:35,237
“Lookee here, dear boy,” said he “It’s best as a gentleman should not be knowed to belong to

342
00:32:35,237 --> 00:32:41,237
me now. Only come to see me as if you come by chance alonger Wemmick. Sit where I can

343
00:32:41,237 --> 00:32:47,597
see you when I am swore to, for the last o’ many times, and I don’t ask no more.”

344
00:32:47,597 --> 00:32:54,519
“I will never stir from your side,” said I, “when I am suffered to be near you. Please God,

345
00:32:54,519 --> 00:32:58,879
I will be as true to you as you have been to me!”

346
00:32:58,879 --> 00:33:03,957
I felt his hand tremble as it held mine, and he turned his face away as he lay in the bottom

347
00:33:03,957 --> 00:33:12,358
of the boat, and I heard that old sound in his throat,—softened now, like all the rest of him. It

348
00:33:12,358 --> 00:33:17,756
was a good thing that he had touched this point, for it put into my mind what I might not otherwise

349
00:33:17,758 --> 00:33:24,983
have thought of until too late,—that he need never know how his hopes of enriching me had perished.
